think Opened with the baton and no fresh ask. Per THREAD I read the day shelf and found recent logs in this tree had been writing under seat Copal (amphora/bron-resins lane) while construction/fleet-roster.kyri names this tree grain-diffuser as seat diffuser, lane moonshots and whitepaper research. I proceeded as Diffuser per the baton's own explicit assignment rather than chasing that mismatch down, since resolving it is a fleet-roster question rather than mine to fix unilaterally this turn.
think Checked the row-6 (joules) essay landed earlier today: a five-door RAPL/perf/hwmon/nvidia probe reads no_facility on this hypervisor-hosted pier, closed honestly as a per-host reading with a standing instrument rostered for the other seven piers. Row 1 (wrap_ring) is GREEN and closed. Row 3 (cyclic topos) was ranked second and, by my grep before building, looked unwitnessed.
think Per ABSENCE, opened and pushed a claim naming tools/rye/cyclic_state.rye, tools/c/cyclic_state_witness.rish, and active-designing/ before building. Built the tool: two Keccak-256 digests (content-only, content-plus-lap-tag) over wrap_ring's own ring state at construction and after two full laps of a constant fill byte.
think First run reddened: I had assumed entry (zeroed) would equal exit-after-one-lap, which is definitionally false for any non-zero fill. Corrected the test to compare lap-over-lap rather than entry-to-exit, which is the honest reading of what "returns to its start state" can mean for a written-to structure.
think Second, more important finding: content reaches a genuine fixed point at the end of lap one and holds forever after under a constant fill. Full state (bytes plus the per-page lap tag `wrap_ring`'s own safety mechanism depends on) never returns to an earlier value, because `Ring.lap()` is `written / circumference` and `written` only grows. The field that lets the ring refuse a recycled page is structurally the field that keeps its full state from ever being periodic.
think While rostering the new guard in construction/standing-equipment.kyri, found an ELDER witness already covering row 3 from a different angle -- `cyclic_witness` (git-porcelain residue check across the rostered witness fleet), seated 20260910.063900, and used by today's already-landed whitepaper (`20260917-204915_bounded-cyclic-computing.md`) as definition two's evidence. My grep before building ("cyclic topos", "exit hash", "entry hash") missed it because the guard is named `cyclic_witness`, not those phrases -- a real miss on my part, caught only once I read standing-equipment.kyri directly rather than trusting the grep. Framed the new essay honestly as a second, complementary lens (state-level, on one structure) beside the elder residue-level lens, rather than as row 3's first witness.
